hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to be  talking about how the process of  translation differs in prokaryotes and  eukaryotes make sure you understand that  translation is that final step in gene  expression where you have genetic  information that's stored in DNA and  then it is expressed through a way of an  mRNA intermediate which is used to make  protein in a process of translation so  now let's get started talking about  translation in prokaryotes versus  eukaryotes  prokaryotes we're talking about bacteria  and archaea eukaryotes we're talking  about things like animals and plants and  fungi and algae and so forth  so translation occurs after  transcription in eukaryotes her  transcription is making the RNA using  DNA as a template so making that RNA  intermediate so translation occurs after  transcription in eukaryotes but at the  same time as transcription in  prokaryotes this is referred to as  coupled transcription and translation  where they're occurring simultaneously  the lifespan of the mRNA transcript is  quite short and prokaryotes and much  longer and eukaryotes with prokaryotes  their mRNA usually only lasts a few  seconds or maybe a few minutes whereas  with eukaryotes the mRNA lasted much  longer amount of time we're talking like  hours or even days prokaryotes and  eukaryotes also have different ribosomes  that they use remember that ribosomes  are the cellular machinery where the  mRNA is used as a template to build  proteins prokaryotes use 70s ribosomes  eukaryotes use 80s ribosomes here the s  is a spend burg unit I'll write that  right here fed burg and it's just a  sedimentation  measure so basically eukaryotes have 80s  ribosomes they sediment a little faster  when they're centrifuge procures of  these 70s ribosomes they don't sediment  as quickly there I'm a little bit  lighter essentially I remember that  ribosomes are composed of protein  subunits and also ribosomal RNA and so  not only are these sizes different but  the actual ribosomal proteins and  ribosomal RNA that make up these  ribosomes are different in prokaryotes  versus eukaryotes prokaryotes and  eukaryotes do use the same start codon a  u G but upstream of that start codon  prokaryotes have a special sequence it's  a AGG AGG remember a is adenine G is  guanine and this sequence is called the  shine-dalgarno sequence so named for a  couple of scientists the shine-dalgarno  sequence helps to anchor the 70s  ribosome to the mRNA in prokaryotes this  sequence is absent and eukaryotes so  eukaryotes don't have a shine-dalgarno  sequence but they still have ways to  anchor their 80s ribosomes to their mRNA  and that is through using the five prime  cap and something called a Kozak  sequence the Kozak sequence is not as  rigid as this AGG AGG there's a few  amino acids that have to be the same but  some of the amino acids in and around  those set amino acids are variable and  so I doesn't have like a very defined  sequence the way that the shine-dalgarno  sequence does prokaryotic mRNA also  could have multiple start sites this is  called polycistronic  so polycistronic just means that  multiple genes can be on one mRNA  transcript or the information from  multiple genes and so that encodes  multiple polypeptides or proteins and  polypeptides and proteins are the same  thing so prokaryotic mRNA may have only  one start site and only code for one  protein but it can also have many  whereas eukaryotic mRNA only ever has  one and that is called mono so Stronach  so where one transcript results in one  polypeptide and that's always true for  eukaryotic mRNA prokaryotes and  eukaryotes also have different types and  numbers of elongation and termination  factors so initiation factors are small  proteins that help get translation  started elongation factors are proteins  that make sure that it keeps going and  termination factors are proteins that  help translation and at the appropriate  time and so prokaryotes and eukaryotes  have different types of these factors  and also different numbers of these  factors prokaryotic translation is also  a lot faster prokaryotes can assemble  roughly twenty amino acids per second  remember that amino acids are the  building blocks of proteins eukaryotic  translation on the other hand is quite a  bit slower averaging at only one amino  acid per second and also the initiator  tRNA in proteins carry the initiator  tRNA and prokaryotes carries a different  amino acid or modified amino acid  relative to eukaryotes specifically  eukaryotes the very first tRNA that  comes into the ribosome at the start of  translation to bring what's going to be  the first amino acid  the polypeptide it's always carrying an  amino acid known as Metheny and this is  what methionine looks like  however the initiator tRNA in  prokaryotes is carrying a modified  version of methionine called a formyl  methionine and formyl methionine  basically just has this formal group  that's shown right here that makes it  different from the methionine that's  seen and eukaryotes and that's seen in  other parts of the prokaryotic  polypeptide this formal group Toth is at  the very start of the prokaryotic  polypeptide it does get removed when  translation is over prokaryotes and  eukaryotes also use the same Universal  genetic code what that means is that in  mRNA there are codons which are sets of  three non-overlapping nucleotides that  code for specific amino acids so for  example the start codon Aug always codes  for methionine it's going to be this  kind of methionine and eukaryotes and  this kind of methionine and prokaryotes  that are always close from a tiny and  then there's other sets of triplets of  three nucleotides that code for  different amino acids and that code is  the same and prokaryotes and new periods  that's why you can take a gene from a  human like for human insulin put it into  a bacterium and the bacterium can then  make and I protein identical to what the  human cells made because we all use that  same universal genetic code and we all  use the same 20 amino acids but some  prokaryotes have two additional amino  acids and those are Celino cysteine  and pyrrole I seen so some prokaryotes  will have Cellino cysteine and Pyrrha  lysine in some of their polypeptides  that eukaryotes are just sticking to the  other 20 amino acids if you're  interested in learning more about  couples transcription and translation in  prokaryotes you can see my video on that  topic you can also see my video on the  central dogma of molecular biology which  explains how information travels from  DNA through that RNA intermediate into a  polypeptide I've also got a nice video  on this universal genetic code  explaining how it's used and how mRNA is  read to build polypeptides and then I've  also got a video looking at the process  of transcription and how it differs in  prokaryotes and eukaryotes so feel free  to check out those videos and thanks for  watching biology professor I'll see you  next time
 

